# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# Dependencies
/node_modules
node_modules/
# package-lock.json is COMMITTED: it is what makes installs reproducible and what lets CI use
# `npm ci`. Without it every install re-resolves within the package.json ranges, so a clean
# `npm audit` can silently drift on the next install.
pnpm-lock.yaml
yarn.lock
/target/

# Next.js
/.next/
/out/

# Build outputs
/build
/dist
out/
bin/
*.AppImage
*.deb
*.rpm
# The packaged desktop binary. Four copies of it (~87 MB each) reached git history before this rule
# existed and are still the bulk of the clone; nothing should add a fifth.
/release/

# Maven
target/
pom.xml.tag
pom.xml.releaseBackup
pom.xml.versionsBackup
pom.xml.next
release.properties
dependency-reduced-pom.xml
buildNumber.properties
.mvn/timing.properties
.mvn/wrapper/maven-wrapper.jar

# Java
*.class
*.jar
!target/*.jar
*.war
*.ear
hs_err_pid*
replay_pid*

# IDE
.vscode/
.idea/
.claude/
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# Logs
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
*.log

# Ad-hoc test and debug scripts.
#
# NOT a blanket `*.sh`. That rule was here, and it silently excluded `tools/refresh/run.sh` — which
# `package.json`'s `update-data` invokes — so the data pipeline was broken on every fresh clone while
# working perfectly for anyone who already had the file. Nothing failed locally, which is exactly why
# it survived. Match the throwaways by name instead of by extension.
test-*.sh
debug-*.sh
test-*.html
debug_*.html

# Phase-0 gate against the hardcoded Java engine, which no longer exists in this repo (`src/main/java`
# is gone and there is no pom.xml). It cannot run, so it is not shipped. `tools/extractor` IS tracked
# because its DataExtractor.java and README are, and a tracked tool missing its runner is worse than
# either alternative.
/tools/roundtrip/
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# Environment files
.env*
!.env.example

